WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.458 --> 00:00:02.815
- Great movies have been
made out of mediocre scripts

00:00:02.815 --> 00:00:05.702
and not great movies have been
made out of great scripts.

00:00:05.702 --> 00:00:08.248
If you have any wisdom
whatsoever, you know to follow,

00:00:08.248 --> 00:00:10.589
you know, wherever there is great energy.

00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:13.055
Adulthood is about compromising.

00:00:13.055 --> 00:00:15.083
It's all about compromising
and how much you compromise

00:00:15.083 --> 00:00:16.712
before you compromise yourself.

00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:18.180
You have to work hard,

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.207
like freedom is one the
other side of discipline.

00:00:20.207 --> 00:00:22.850
- He's an American actor
who began acting as a child

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.607
with his film debut in
City Slickers in 1991.

00:00:26.607 --> 00:00:30.081
His breakout role was in the
cult movie hit, Donnie Darko.

00:00:30.081 --> 00:00:33.043
For Nightcrawler, his
performance was widely acclaimed

00:00:33.043 --> 00:00:36.006
and won great rave reviews from critics.

00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:38.090
He's Jake Gyllenhaal, and here's my take

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.752
on his top ten rules for success.

00:00:39.752 --> 00:00:41.638
Rules number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:41.638 --> 00:00:43.539
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:44.637
for some special bonus clips,

00:00:44.637 --> 00:00:46.136
and if there's something that Jake says

00:00:46.136 --> 00:00:47.761
that really inspires
you as you're watching,

00:00:47.761 --> 00:00:49.589
make sure to leave it in
the comments with quotes

00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:51.549
so other people can benefit as well.

00:00:51.549 --> 00:00:54.613
(whooshing)

00:00:54.613 --> 00:00:57.565
- Someone said to me once
that like a, and I know that

00:00:57.565 --> 00:00:59.086
my mother being a screenwriter,

00:00:59.086 --> 00:01:00.731
she's not going to agree with me on this,

00:01:00.731 --> 00:01:02.873
but that, you know, great
movies have been made

00:01:02.873 --> 00:01:05.315
out of mediocre scripts,
and not great movies

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:08.114
have been made out of
great scripts, you know?

00:01:08.114 --> 00:01:09.890
There is something in that process,

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.304
'cause there is a general
kind of philosophy

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:14.531
that you need a great
script, which I agree with.

00:01:14.531 --> 00:01:16.640
I totally agree with, but ultimately,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.286
I think you need an energy.

00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:20.809
You need a drive and a
belief in what you're doing,

00:01:20.809 --> 00:01:23.296
and that belief, whatever that is,

00:01:23.296 --> 00:01:24.963
can change an entire

00:01:27.765 --> 00:01:28.598
film.

00:01:28.598 --> 00:01:29.980
It can change anything, really.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.063
What I was always taught as a kid was

00:01:33.903 --> 00:01:36.676
to try and listen to myself

00:01:36.676 --> 00:01:40.093
and to trust certain instincts, you know?

00:01:42.085 --> 00:01:46.725
To listen to people around
me and to use those instincts

00:01:46.725 --> 00:01:48.781
to try and guide me, you know?

00:01:48.781 --> 00:01:51.414
And I think, you know,

00:01:51.414 --> 00:01:54.140
you get, you're on
course, you're off course,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:55.730
you move around, you're all different,

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.593
you try this path, you try
that path, whatever it is,

00:01:57.593 --> 00:02:01.510
but I think I've just
become much clearer about

00:02:03.175 --> 00:02:04.100
my instinct,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.552
and particularly my initial instincts.

00:02:05.552 --> 00:02:07.250
I'm not talking about like oh,

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.587
that person seems like a nice...

00:02:08.587 --> 00:02:11.158
I'm talking about the
instinct when we talk about

00:02:11.158 --> 00:02:12.771
reading the script for this movie,

00:02:12.771 --> 00:02:15.296
and I all of a sudden
thought this guy is a coyote.

00:02:15.296 --> 00:02:18.007
It came to my mind, and I
thought he needs to be hungry.

00:02:18.007 --> 00:02:21.366
You know, things work in the
moment and then can die off,

00:02:21.366 --> 00:02:23.946
and then things can not work
in one moment and come to life,

00:02:23.946 --> 00:02:26.011
and if you have any wisdom whatsoever,

00:02:26.011 --> 00:02:27.474
you know to follow, you know,

00:02:27.474 --> 00:02:29.997
wherever there is great energy,

00:02:29.997 --> 00:02:33.237
and that's what I followed
with Denis when we did Enemy.

00:02:33.237 --> 00:02:35.063
I went this guy has great energy.

00:02:35.063 --> 00:02:38.104
We vibe on each other,
and I got to go with this.

00:02:38.104 --> 00:02:41.282
Like I don't know what this movie's about,

00:02:41.282 --> 00:02:42.998
but I know this energy.

00:02:42.998 --> 00:02:44.287
- [George] Right.

00:02:44.287 --> 00:02:46.528
- And I know this energy
is success somewhere,

00:02:46.528 --> 00:02:49.965
creative success, and that's
first and foremost to me,

00:02:49.965 --> 00:02:52.013
and that's what I follow now,

00:02:52.013 --> 00:02:53.717
and that energy drives a movie.

00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:55.209
When you watch it as an
audience, it's infectious.

00:02:55.209 --> 00:02:57.331
When you see that on the screen,

00:02:57.331 --> 00:02:59.422
and I see it all the time with people,

00:02:59.422 --> 00:03:02.530
you're infected by that energy.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:06.276
I think I just decided
should try not to compromise,

00:03:06.276 --> 00:03:09.120
you know, certain things.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.370
You know, one of these things,
and it's funny to bring up,

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:16.501
my publicist, because she
was the one who recommended

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:19.365
this documentary to me,
but in this documentary

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.401
that I watched a few
years ago, The Promise,

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:22.864
the one about Bruce Springsteen,

00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:25.952
you know, he said that
adulthood is about compromising.

00:03:25.952 --> 00:03:27.744
It's all about compromise
and how much you compromise

00:03:27.744 --> 00:03:30.209
before you compromise yourself,

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:34.535
and I think that when I
say there is no compromise

00:03:34.535 --> 00:03:37.857
in the choices I made, I don't
know if that's totally true,

00:03:37.857 --> 00:03:42.024
but I think it was sort of
not compromising myself,

00:03:43.052 --> 00:03:45.469
you know, and trying to be as

00:03:46.805 --> 00:03:49.699
mindful in decisions I
was making as possible,

00:03:49.699 --> 00:03:51.068
not only in the movies,

00:03:51.068 --> 00:03:52.995
but also within scenes and everything.

00:03:52.995 --> 00:03:54.822
- [David] The weird thing
about becoming a movie star,

00:03:54.822 --> 00:03:56.269
it just kind of happened.

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.419
What about yourself?

00:03:57.419 --> 00:03:59.009
- Strangely, it does sort of happen,

00:03:59.009 --> 00:04:02.441
like in your 20s, which
is when a majority of

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:06.321
all that stuff started to
happen was I think you just

00:04:06.321 --> 00:04:08.479
sort of, you do the best you can.

00:04:08.479 --> 00:04:10.125
Listen to yourself, but you also

00:04:10.125 --> 00:04:12.356
follow different people's leads
and try and learn from it,

00:04:12.356 --> 00:04:14.548
so I mean, that sort
of ended up happening,

00:04:14.548 --> 00:04:16.728
and I sort of hit 30 and
I was like wait a second.

00:04:16.728 --> 00:04:18.463
I want to get back on stage again

00:04:18.463 --> 00:04:21.204
and do this thing that
I love doing so much,

00:04:21.204 --> 00:04:22.987
and it just happened.

00:04:22.987 --> 00:04:24.680
- [David] Well, all of your
choices lately, actually,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.374
as for film as well, I mean,
are we in a post-ab phase

00:04:28.374 --> 00:04:30.443
of Jake Gyllenhaal's career?

00:04:30.443 --> 00:04:31.276
Is that?

00:04:31.276 --> 00:04:34.722
- Aptly put, or should I say ab-tly put.

00:04:34.722 --> 00:04:35.555
I think,

00:04:37.337 --> 00:04:38.551
yes and no.

00:04:38.551 --> 00:04:40.061
I mean, I don't know.

00:04:40.061 --> 00:04:42.287
Yeah, I guess if it's about,

00:04:42.287 --> 00:04:44.319
to me, if it ends up being about vanity

00:04:44.319 --> 00:04:47.008
or something like that, then
it's just the wrong direction,

00:04:47.008 --> 00:04:49.773
you know, and I think
ultimately if it's about

00:04:49.773 --> 00:04:52.222
what you feel and what moves you

00:04:52.222 --> 00:04:53.906
and what roles you want to play

00:04:53.906 --> 00:04:55.217
or what people you want to work with.

00:04:55.217 --> 00:04:57.392
If those are the choices
and that's how it's done,

00:04:57.392 --> 00:04:59.684
then I think, you know,
that's what fulfills me,

00:04:59.684 --> 00:05:01.184
so whether it's post or not,

00:05:01.184 --> 00:05:02.864
I think I always was doing that, you know?

00:05:02.864 --> 00:05:07.196
My intention always, whenever
I go into any creative

00:05:07.196 --> 00:05:10.529
sort of environment or experience, is to

00:05:12.689 --> 00:05:14.365
give my all, you know?

00:05:14.365 --> 00:05:16.948
I mean, I care a lot, you know?

00:05:18.118 --> 00:05:20.546
And it always has been like that.

00:05:20.546 --> 00:05:22.599
So let me just say, the one thing I do

00:05:22.599 --> 00:05:24.516
really, really know now

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:29.161
is how blessed I am to be
working in this business,

00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:31.719
and that you have to work hard.

00:05:31.719 --> 00:05:34.756
Like freedom is on the
other side of discipline,

00:05:34.756 --> 00:05:38.504
and when somebody says I want you to play

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:41.611
this role in my movie, you
have a great responsibility,

00:05:41.611 --> 00:05:44.082
and it's just bottom line for me.

00:05:44.082 --> 00:05:46.731
And from here on out,
and has been for a while,

00:05:46.731 --> 00:05:49.181
that is I will do
everything I can for that,

00:05:49.181 --> 00:05:51.226
so as long as the opportunities come,

00:05:51.226 --> 00:05:53.026
I will do my best and try my best

00:05:53.026 --> 00:05:55.858
to do the best that I
can do at that moment.

00:05:55.858 --> 00:05:58.080
- Do you care about the
business end of things

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.311
when you pick gigs now?

00:05:59.311 --> 00:06:01.762
In terms of this what seems
to be a highly curatorial

00:06:01.762 --> 00:06:03.062
approach to what you do,

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:06.851
does it matter if your
agent goes hey, man,

00:06:06.851 --> 00:06:10.206
you really should do
this role in Iron Man,

00:06:10.206 --> 00:06:12.937
because it's going to
be big business for you?

00:06:12.937 --> 00:06:16.134
- If my agent did that, we
wouldn't be working together.

00:06:16.134 --> 00:06:17.033
- Really?

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:19.450
- Yeah, because I think that,

00:06:21.928 --> 00:06:25.570
you know, to me, we're not talking about.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:28.070
We're talking about, you know,

00:06:30.382 --> 00:06:33.032
I mean, I'm incredibly blessed, you know,

00:06:33.032 --> 00:06:36.306
in the work that I do in
terms of that kind of stuff.

00:06:36.306 --> 00:06:38.558
I mean, that's just not the discussion

00:06:38.558 --> 00:06:39.927
that I want to be having.

00:06:39.927 --> 00:06:40.760
You know?

00:06:43.218 --> 00:06:46.363
That's just, at this point in my life,

00:06:46.363 --> 00:06:47.530
I believe that

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.117
I want to stay true to a certain instinct.

00:06:51.117 --> 00:06:53.148
Now, that instinct changes
at times, you know?

00:06:53.148 --> 00:06:56.431
And it will change over the course of,

00:06:56.431 --> 00:06:58.822
hopefully, I get to work more, you know?

00:06:58.822 --> 00:07:02.747
And hopefully I'll be
making different choices

00:07:02.747 --> 00:07:05.010
and I might come in
here two years from now

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:08.071
and have a different
approach, but I do know that

00:07:08.071 --> 00:07:10.323
it should always be about
something that's coming

00:07:10.323 --> 00:07:11.603
from something I believe in,

00:07:11.603 --> 00:07:13.285
'cause that's all that I can bring.

00:07:13.285 --> 00:07:15.310
You know, it's really
all that I can bring,

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:16.811
and it's going to satisfy some people

00:07:16.811 --> 00:07:18.055
and it's not going to others,

00:07:18.055 --> 00:07:20.233
just as things other people do satisfy me

00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:22.569
and some people don't, you know?

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:24.447
But if we have that discussion,

00:07:24.447 --> 00:07:25.614
to me, I just,

00:07:27.327 --> 00:07:31.785
you know, it's not one I
necessarily want to have.

00:07:31.785 --> 00:07:34.784
To me, it's become much more about craft,

00:07:34.784 --> 00:07:37.030
and I think, actually, on a smaller scale,

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:39.318
I think it's possibly to do
it on a really big scale,

00:07:39.318 --> 00:07:40.943
but I think that, on a smaller scale,

00:07:40.943 --> 00:07:43.101
I think preparation is key.

00:07:43.101 --> 00:07:47.343
That I kind of went back to
deciding or sort of choosing

00:07:47.343 --> 00:07:51.497
preparation over everything
and the amount of time it takes

00:07:51.497 --> 00:07:53.968
when you have a lot less
money to use, you know,

00:07:53.968 --> 00:07:55.922
and you have to make
choices very specifically,

00:07:55.922 --> 00:07:59.652
and they have to be kind of
prepared much earlier on,

00:07:59.652 --> 00:08:01.993
so that you're free in, you know,

00:08:01.993 --> 00:08:04.318
a very small period of space.

00:08:04.318 --> 00:08:06.658
You have a lot more freedom
because of all the preparation

00:08:06.658 --> 00:08:07.573
that you've done.

00:08:07.573 --> 00:08:11.667
I mean, just in terms of
the process of acting,

00:08:11.667 --> 00:08:12.750
that's what I

00:08:14.412 --> 00:08:17.645
started to learn when I did End of Watch,

00:08:17.645 --> 00:08:18.983
this movie with David Ayer.

00:08:18.983 --> 00:08:21.997
You know, I spent five
months with police officers,

00:08:21.997 --> 00:08:23.606
and that was the beginning of realizing

00:08:23.606 --> 00:08:25.581
how much I loved preparation

00:08:25.581 --> 00:08:29.420
and how much I loved the
process before the performance,

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:32.530
and I had always loved
that when I was on stage

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.257
because there's always that
very formal rehearsal process

00:08:35.257 --> 00:08:37.634
that allows you to
explore and use your mind

00:08:37.634 --> 00:08:39.693
and go to places that are wrong

00:08:39.693 --> 00:08:41.803
as well as places that are right,

00:08:41.803 --> 00:08:45.403
and I love being wrong
and right, you know?

00:08:45.403 --> 00:08:47.817
And I love people saying hmm,

00:08:47.817 --> 00:08:49.666
that idea doesn't really work for this,

00:08:49.666 --> 00:08:52.169
you know, and I'm having an
exchange with the director.

00:08:52.169 --> 00:08:53.266
I love that.

00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:56.392
I love that kind of I'm bringing an idea

00:08:56.392 --> 00:08:58.553
and it gets vetoed or it gets accepted.

00:08:58.553 --> 00:09:01.230
Whatever it is, it's just
the ability to bring ideas,

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:04.347
and I see that it's much
more possible to do that

00:09:04.347 --> 00:09:09.174
within the prep time than it
is in the moment of shooting.

00:09:09.174 --> 00:09:12.408
I was thinking about this the other day.

00:09:12.408 --> 00:09:15.355
I was thinking about
working with Dustin Hoffman

00:09:15.355 --> 00:09:16.188
when I was

00:09:17.696 --> 00:09:21.196
20 years old or 23 or something like that,

00:09:22.325 --> 00:09:24.186
and I remember Dustin coming up to me

00:09:24.186 --> 00:09:26.070
and him saying to me, "You know you don't

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:29.529
"have to do so much," and I was like what?

00:09:29.529 --> 00:09:32.674
He said, "You know you carry
everything inside you already.

00:09:32.674 --> 00:09:35.231
"Like you could probably just stand there

00:09:35.231 --> 00:09:38.670
"and there's enough that
people could watch, you know?"

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.357
And that was expressed,
actually, while we were doing

00:09:41.357 --> 00:09:43.825
this movie I just did in
a way that I really like,

00:09:43.825 --> 00:09:47.642
which is just deliver the pizza, you know?

00:09:47.642 --> 00:09:51.070
And that's one of the
biggest things I learned,

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:54.403
which is, you know, we all carry with us

00:09:55.356 --> 00:09:57.665
like everything we do,

00:09:57.665 --> 00:10:00.798
like everything we've been
through, good, bad, whatever,

00:10:00.798 --> 00:10:05.123
and if you can show up and
literally just deliver the pizza,

00:10:05.123 --> 00:10:07.615
like you'll be alright.

00:10:07.615 --> 00:10:10.247
(laughter)

00:10:10.247 --> 00:10:12.903
Because you're not good, like
he said in a calm tone about,

00:10:12.903 --> 00:10:14.966
you know, just speaking poetry, you know?

00:10:14.966 --> 00:10:17.453
You know, you don't
have to be the butterfly

00:10:17.453 --> 00:10:18.953
flying around, you know?

00:10:18.953 --> 00:10:21.863
Just say the word butterfly, you know?

00:10:21.863 --> 00:10:24.494
Just say it, because
people will take from that

00:10:24.494 --> 00:10:26.138
their own experience,

00:10:26.138 --> 00:10:28.573
because if you don't give
credit to the audience

00:10:28.573 --> 00:10:30.271
and how intelligent they are

00:10:30.271 --> 00:10:32.631
and how much they're going
to be asking already,

00:10:32.631 --> 00:10:35.610
then you're done, you know?

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:40.014
I have just learned to have
great respect for the audience,

00:10:40.014 --> 00:10:44.181
and I love them, and I will
do anything for their minds.

00:10:45.323 --> 00:10:48.337
I know that sounds
crazy, but I really will.

00:10:48.337 --> 00:10:50.223
- You seem like a pretty ambitious guy.

00:10:50.223 --> 00:10:52.710
Now one does as much as you've done

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.957
without being an ambitious guy.

00:10:54.957 --> 00:10:56.879
Do you, or does Jake
Gyllenhaal, relate at all

00:10:56.879 --> 00:10:59.439
to that notion of success at any cost?

00:10:59.439 --> 00:11:03.326
Was that drilled into you at some point?

00:11:03.326 --> 00:11:04.523
- I don't know.

00:11:04.523 --> 00:11:07.453
I mean, consciously speaking, no.

00:11:07.453 --> 00:11:08.370
I mean, no,

00:11:09.226 --> 00:11:11.969
and I think what I value
as success as I get older

00:11:11.969 --> 00:11:14.069
is a lot different than what it was

00:11:14.069 --> 00:11:16.669
when I was a lot younger, you know?

00:11:16.669 --> 00:11:18.402
- [Jian] What's the difference?

00:11:18.402 --> 00:11:21.054
- Well, I mean, I think an
immature view of success,

00:11:21.054 --> 00:11:23.844
you know, which I probably
can be still guilty of,

00:11:23.844 --> 00:11:25.656
is all of the things that, you know,

00:11:25.656 --> 00:11:29.406
that you get the things
you sort of dream of,

00:11:30.621 --> 00:11:32.204
you know, money and

00:11:34.013 --> 00:11:36.194
more work, and the work that you wanted,

00:11:36.194 --> 00:11:37.291
all of those things.

00:11:37.291 --> 00:11:40.177
And I think I now value success
as a much different thing.

00:11:40.177 --> 00:11:42.010
I think I value it as,

00:11:43.224 --> 00:11:45.449
you know, when I see my friends

00:11:45.449 --> 00:11:46.748
with their beautiful children

00:11:46.748 --> 00:11:48.999
or in a working relationship of any kind

00:11:48.999 --> 00:11:50.969
and all those things, to
me, that's real success,

00:11:50.969 --> 00:11:53.802
so I mean, I think that, you know,

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:58.191
when I have like a
great day with my family

00:11:58.191 --> 00:11:59.971
or a great day with my nieces or whatever,

00:11:59.971 --> 00:12:01.376
that's a successful day, you know?

00:12:01.376 --> 00:12:03.057
I could come to the Toronto Film Festival

00:12:03.057 --> 00:12:05.984
and people could really love,
you know, a film that I made,

00:12:05.984 --> 00:12:08.256
and that's successful,
but it's not necessarily

00:12:08.256 --> 00:12:12.256
how I would now judge
success of a certain kind.

00:12:13.173 --> 00:12:14.647
- Is that because you sowed your oats?

00:12:14.647 --> 00:12:15.953
I mean, is it really?

00:12:15.953 --> 00:12:19.392
Is it like did you want
Entourage at some point,

00:12:19.392 --> 00:12:21.180
and then you got it?

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:23.612
I don't know.
- I don't know about that.

00:12:23.612 --> 00:12:25.331
I think I just credit it to my parents,

00:12:25.331 --> 00:12:29.490
and you know, hopefully
values that they instill.

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:31.617
(whooshing)

00:12:31.617 --> 00:12:32.990
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:35.863
I made this video because
NoName11 asked me to,

00:12:35.863 --> 00:12:38.326
so if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:12:38.326 --> 00:12:40.325
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:12:40.325 --> 00:12:41.474
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:41.474 --> 00:12:43.120
I'd also love to know
which of the 10 rules

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:45.037
had the biggest impact on you and why,

00:12:45.037 --> 00:12:46.938
what change you're going to
make to your business or life

00:12:46.938 --> 00:12:48.895
as a result of watching this video?

00:12:48.895 --> 00:12:51.699
Leave it in the comments and
I will join in the discussion.

00:12:51.699 --> 00:12:53.308
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:12:53.308 --> 00:12:54.311
to Alexis Daroussos.

00:12:54.311 --> 00:12:56.432
Alexis, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book.

00:12:56.432 --> 00:12:57.843
Really means a lot to me.

00:12:57.843 --> 00:12:58.992
Thank you for that support.

00:12:58.992 --> 00:13:00.817
For those of you watching, you
want a chance at a shout out

00:13:00.817 --> 00:13:03.621
in a future video, make sure
to grab a copy of the book

00:13:03.621 --> 00:13:06.123
and email in your receipt
so we can keep track.

00:13:06.123 --> 00:13:07.733
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:07.733 --> 00:13:10.475
Continue to believe or
whatever your one word is,

00:13:10.475 --> 00:13:12.361
and I'll see you soon.

00:13:12.361 --> 00:13:14.694
(whooshing)

00:13:18.411 --> 00:13:21.161
(distant sirens)

00:13:22.581 --> 00:13:24.848
- Come on, marry me?

00:13:24.848 --> 00:13:27.883
- Okay, how about let
me check my schedule.

00:13:27.883 --> 00:13:29.273
I'll get back to you.

00:13:29.273 --> 00:13:30.824
- Hold my hand.

00:13:30.824 --> 00:13:32.738
I'll be waiting for you.

00:13:32.738 --> 00:13:35.283
- [Bartender] Uh-huh.

00:13:35.283 --> 00:13:36.990
- What's up, man?

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.799
Thought you said you didn't drink?

00:13:38.799 --> 00:13:41.033
- What, you think a man
can't pick up a new habit?

00:13:41.033 --> 00:13:42.562
What you doing here, man?

00:13:42.562 --> 00:13:44.727
Seriously, this is my spot.

00:13:44.727 --> 00:13:46.133
- Can I get a water?

00:13:46.133 --> 00:13:47.461
- Sure.
- Can I have some water?

00:13:47.461 --> 00:13:49.628
- Drink is a solitary sport.

00:13:49.628 --> 00:13:51.767
It's a solitary one.

00:13:51.767 --> 00:13:54.988
- Man, I'm just trying to connect here.

00:13:54.988 --> 00:13:55.821
- I just.

00:13:56.926 --> 00:13:58.386
- I just didn't want to go home.

00:13:58.386 --> 00:14:01.469
300 square feet of nothing, you know?

00:14:06.236 --> 00:14:07.569
- You got plans?

00:14:09.162 --> 00:14:10.625
- I don't know what you mean?

00:14:10.625 --> 00:14:12.103
- You know what you're going
to do with all this training

00:14:12.103 --> 00:14:14.159
that you think you're
so desperately needing?

00:14:14.159 --> 00:14:15.672
What you going to do with it?

00:14:15.672 --> 00:14:17.037
- I'm going to fight,

00:14:17.037 --> 00:14:18.228
and then I'm going to get my kid back.

00:14:18.228 --> 00:14:20.020
- Then you going to
end up right back here?

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:20.950
- What are you saying?

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:24.343
- What do you think got you here?

00:14:24.343 --> 00:14:26.628
- Some scumbag killed my wife.

00:14:26.628 --> 00:14:28.472
- What do you think you did

00:14:28.472 --> 00:14:30.055
to be sitting here?

00:14:33.409 --> 00:14:35.384
Why did your wife get killed?

00:14:35.384 --> 00:14:37.672
- Look, man, all due respect, you know,

00:14:37.672 --> 00:14:39.610
I haven't really gotten much sleep, man.

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:40.598
What are you trying to ask me?

00:14:40.598 --> 00:14:41.491
- What happened?

00:14:41.491 --> 00:14:42.588
- I don't, I just told you what happened.

00:14:42.588 --> 00:14:43.816
- What did you do?

00:14:43.816 --> 00:14:45.154
- Why do you keep asking me that question?

00:14:45.154 --> 00:14:47.090
- What happened?

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:48.257
What happened?

00:14:48.257 --> 00:14:49.882
- I just told you what happened.

00:14:49.882 --> 00:14:50.779
- Why?

00:14:50.779 --> 00:14:51.862
- I can't tell you why.

00:14:51.862 --> 00:14:53.032
What do you mean, why?

00:14:53.032 --> 00:14:54.032
- Nevermind.

00:14:55.024 --> 00:14:57.640
Can't even hear the question.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:58.534
- What are you laughing at?

00:14:58.534 --> 00:14:59.646
What, is this funny to you?

00:14:59.646 --> 00:15:01.020
- You can't even like even.
- That's funny to you?

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.139
- Whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:15:03.139 --> 00:15:05.210
- Piece of shh.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:06.104
- See that?

00:15:06.104 --> 00:15:07.917
That's what, that's why you're here.

00:15:07.917 --> 00:15:09.721
That's why you're here.

00:15:09.721 --> 00:15:10.707
- It's all good, Tick?

00:15:10.707 --> 00:15:12.874
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, it is.

00:15:13.951 --> 00:15:17.356
(horns blowing)

00:15:17.356 --> 00:15:18.606
- It's all good.

00:15:18.606 --> 00:15:20.424
- Yo, I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

00:15:20.424 --> 00:15:21.939
- [Bartender] Hey, man, don't
bring that shh back in here.

00:15:21.939 --> 00:15:23.021
- I'm sorry, man, that's it.

00:15:23.021 --> 00:15:24.154
If I broke anything.
- No, it's okay.

00:15:24.154 --> 00:15:25.504
We don't need it.

00:15:25.504 --> 00:15:27.671
- Nobody wants your money.

00:15:30.769 --> 00:15:33.423
What do you want, man?

00:15:33.423 --> 00:15:35.446
- I never really had any plans, alright?

00:15:35.446 --> 00:15:38.485
My wife always made the plans for me,

00:15:38.485 --> 00:15:42.758
and my daughter doesn't even
want to see me right now.

00:15:42.758 --> 00:15:45.341
(solemn music)

00:15:49.154 --> 00:15:51.987
And I feel like I broke her heart.

00:15:58.857 --> 00:15:59.920
- Look, kids, man.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:02.567
She's not a little girl.

00:16:02.567 --> 00:16:05.650
She's trying to take care of herself.

00:16:06.503 --> 00:16:08.586
She lost her mother, too.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:15.903
Even if she hates you, you
got to let her hate you

00:16:15.903 --> 00:16:18.918
so she can feel better about
it, so she can get better.

00:16:18.918 --> 00:16:21.273
This ain't about how you feel.

00:16:21.273 --> 00:16:25.356
I mean, you've got to let
her go through her thing

00:16:26.859 --> 00:16:30.109
and not think that thing is your thing.

00:16:34.698 --> 00:16:37.948
Then you can deal with like that stuff.

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:42.873
Like boxing, whatever you want.

00:16:46.257 --> 00:16:48.257
(sighs)

00:16:51.226 --> 00:16:54.738
(liquor trickles)

00:16:54.738 --> 00:16:57.352
- Well, at least I don't have to worry

00:16:57.352 --> 00:16:59.352
about losing you to PSP.

00:17:06.298 --> 00:17:08.395
When forensics is finished
with the guy's place,

00:17:08.395 --> 00:17:12.150
we're going to deploy the cadaver dogs.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:16.408
Look, kid, we can't always
save the day, alright?

00:17:16.408 --> 00:17:18.575
We're just cops, janitors.

00:17:20.668 --> 00:17:23.210
So you lost this one, alright?

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:24.307
Look, you want fulfillment?

00:17:24.307 --> 00:17:26.193
You need to find a girl, you know?

00:17:26.193 --> 00:17:28.753
Start a family, have some kids.

00:17:28.753 --> 00:17:30.003
Just let it go.

00:17:33.884 --> 00:17:36.551
(desk clatters)

00:17:47.935 --> 00:17:50.768
(keyboard cracks)

00:17:57.112 --> 00:17:59.612
(phone rings)

00:18:25.226 --> 00:18:28.309
(toy truck clatters)

00:18:40.668 --> 00:18:43.168
(phone rings)

00:18:50.616 --> 00:18:52.737
(cell phone rings)

00:18:52.737 --> 00:18:53.570
- Wait.

00:18:59.664 --> 00:19:00.913
Yeah?

00:19:00.913 --> 00:19:02.428
- [Rich] Hey, it's Rich.

00:19:02.428 --> 00:19:04.011
We found something.

00:19:05.326 --> 00:19:07.909
(door squeaks)

00:19:15.887 --> 00:19:17.752
- You understand that you
have been read your rights

00:19:17.752 --> 00:19:19.690
and you're here voluntarily?

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:21.976
- [Louis] That's right, yes.

00:19:21.976 --> 00:19:24.716
- And this is being recorded and filmed?

00:19:24.716 --> 00:19:27.532
- Yes, from the camera up there,

00:19:27.532 --> 00:19:28.699
in wide angle.

00:19:29.982 --> 00:19:30.815
- Mm-hmm.

00:19:34.637 --> 00:19:35.945
Can you tell me how it is you were

00:19:35.945 --> 00:19:39.345
at the China Town Express
tonight and calling 911?

00:19:39.345 --> 00:19:40.447
- Of course.

00:19:40.447 --> 00:19:43.198
It began earlier in the day.

00:19:43.198 --> 00:19:45.198
I was watering my plant.

00:19:46.701 --> 00:19:49.261
I looked out my window at my apartment,

00:19:49.261 --> 00:19:50.891
the one you visited,

00:19:50.891 --> 00:19:54.454
and I saw a dark colored
vehicle with two men inside

00:19:54.454 --> 00:19:55.786
parked across the street.

00:19:55.786 --> 00:19:56.956
I didn't think anything of it,

00:19:56.956 --> 00:19:59.292
otherwise I would have reported it then.

00:19:59.292 --> 00:20:01.366
We began our night.

00:20:01.366 --> 00:20:02.199
My

00:20:04.579 --> 00:20:06.758
employee and myself,

00:20:06.758 --> 00:20:09.791
and around approximately 12:38,

00:20:09.791 --> 00:20:12.630
I looked at my rear view mirror

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:14.788
and I saw what looked like the same men

00:20:14.788 --> 00:20:16.776
in the same vehicle following us.

00:20:16.776 --> 00:20:19.286
I made an evasive maneuver, circled back,

00:20:19.286 --> 00:20:21.109
and tailed them to the restaurant,

00:20:21.109 --> 00:20:22.663
at which point I saw them go inside

00:20:22.663 --> 00:20:26.670
and recognized them as the same
men from the home invasion.

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:28.538
- You told me you didn't see the men,

00:20:28.538 --> 00:20:30.111
that you didn't get a good look at them.

00:20:30.111 --> 00:20:33.455
- Well, it all came back to
me when I saw them going in,

00:20:33.455 --> 00:20:36.730
their shapes and their movements,

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:39.233
and then I saw that one of the
men had a gun under his shirt

00:20:39.233 --> 00:20:42.744
and so I immediately got
my phone and dialed 911.

00:20:42.744 --> 00:20:43.679
- That's your story.

00:20:43.679 --> 00:20:44.630
- That's what happened.

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:46.384
That's why I'm sitting here with you.

00:20:46.384 --> 00:20:48.543
I think that the men saw my footage

00:20:48.543 --> 00:20:50.626
and they tracked me down.

00:20:52.383 --> 00:20:55.032
- [Detective] You want
to know what I think?

00:20:55.032 --> 00:20:56.199
- Yes, please.

00:20:57.357 --> 00:21:00.880
- I think you withheld information.

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:02.712
I think you saw the two
men in the murder house

00:21:02.712 --> 00:21:05.539
in Grenada Hills, and you saw the car,

00:21:05.539 --> 00:21:09.706
and you sat on it to get
something you could film.

00:21:10.719 --> 00:21:13.136
What do you think about that?

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:17.814
- I think that would be a
very unprofessional thing

00:21:17.814 --> 00:21:19.279
for me to do in my business.

00:21:19.279 --> 00:21:21.165
- It would be murder.

00:21:21.165 --> 00:21:23.046
- I can understand why
you're looking into it,

00:21:23.046 --> 00:21:24.456
but I didn't do anything like that,

00:21:24.456 --> 00:21:26.044
nothing that could be considered wrong.

00:21:26.044 --> 00:21:28.347
- You don't fool me for a minute.

00:21:28.347 --> 00:21:29.284
- I'm glad.

00:21:29.284 --> 00:21:32.920
- I think everything you've said is a lie.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.208
- I wish my partner was here
to back up what happened.

00:21:35.208 --> 00:21:36.852
- You left your dead partner.

00:21:36.852 --> 00:21:38.075
- Well, the ambulances arrived.

00:21:38.075 --> 00:21:39.686
They're trained professionals.

00:21:39.686 --> 00:21:42.503
- Yeah, and you seem real
broken up about that.

00:21:42.503 --> 00:21:45.336
- No, he died doing what he loved.

00:21:47.402 --> 00:21:49.319
- You filmed him dying.

00:21:52.031 --> 00:21:52.924
- Hey, that's what I do.

00:21:52.924 --> 00:21:53.985
It's my job.

00:21:53.985 --> 00:21:55.542
I like to say that if you're seeing me,

00:21:55.542 --> 00:21:59.382
you're having the worst day of your life.

00:21:59.382 --> 00:22:00.817
(dynamic music)

